<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaykhusraw II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1246">1246</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ghiyath al-Din Kaykhusraw II, Kay Khusraw II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaykhusraw II was sultan of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> from 1237 to 1246, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a>. His reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babai-revolt">Babai revolt</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> in 1239–1240 and, in 1243, a crushing defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a>, after which the sultanate became a Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and lost its independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii.jpg" alt="Kaykhusraw II" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaykhusraw_II_dirham.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
